{"type": "say", "text": "Thanks, Maya. Let me give you my assessment of the agreement language. The enterprise customer agreements require notice at least 10 business days before disclosure of 'customer-submitted material' to a regulator. In my review, the operative trigger is the disclosure of the customer-submitted material itself — the content — not merely the disclosure of identifying information attached to it. Pseudonymizing customer identifiers removes the privacy concern, but the underlying material that customers submitted is still being disclosed to the regulator. The agreement language doesn't condition the notice obligation on whether identifiers are attached; it's triggered by the substance of what the customer submitted. So in my read, pseudonymization does not eliminate the 10-business-day notice obligation. That means if any of the version 3 evaluation reports, incident logs, or deployment-change records contain customer-submitted material — even with identifiers stripped — we still need to send customer notices now to preserve our ability to produce within the 30-day window. I'd recommend we trigger notices today given the tight timeline. Daniel, as you revise the draft for the narrowed version 3 scope, please flag which specific document categories are likely to contain customer-submitted material so we can scope the notices appropriately."}
```

